We claim, first of all, that $\phi_2=g^*\phi_1$. Indeed, consider the canonical diagram in ${\Delta \mathfrak{G}}$
\[
\xymatrix{
[n']\ar[d]_{\phi_1^*g}
\ar[r]^{g^*\phi_1}&[n]
\ar[d]^g
\\
[n']\ar[r]_{\phi_1}& [n]
}
\]
defining $g^*\phi_1$ and apply the functor $\lambda$ to it. Because of the assumptions on $g$, the resulting diagram
in ${\bf FSet}$ is 
\begin{equation} \xymatrix{
\{0,1,\dots, n'\}
\ar[d]_{\lambda_n(\phi_1^*g)}
\ar[r]^{\phi_2}& \{0,1,\dots, n\}
\ar[d]^{\lambda_n(g)=\sigma_1^{-1}\sigma^2}
\\
\{0,1,\dots, n'\}\ar[r]_{\phi_1}& \{0,1,\dots, n\},
}
\end{equation}
whence $\phi_2=g^*\phi_1$.

Because of the assumptions on $g$, the resulting diagram
in ${\bf FSet}$ is 
\begin{equation} \xymatrix{
\{0,1,\dots, n'\}
\ar[d]_{\lambda_n(\phi_1^*g)}
\ar[r]^{\phi_2}& \{0,1,\dots, n\}
\ar[d]^{\lambda_n(g)=\sigma_1^{-1}\sigma^2}
\\
\{0,1,\dots, n'\}\ar[r]_{\phi_1}& \{0,1,\dots, n\},
}
\end{equation}
whence $\phi_2=g^*\phi_1$.

By a $G$-{\em structured circle}\footnote{A more precise term would be ``${ \operatorname{Homeo}}^G(S^1)$-structured circle".}
we mean a pair $(C,\rho)$ where $C$ is a circle, and $\rho: F\to{ \operatorname{Homeo}}(S^1, C)$ is a reduction of the structure
group along $p_{ \operatorname{Homeo}}$. Thus, $F$ is a ${ \operatorname{Homeo}}^G(S^1)$-torsor and $\rho$ is $p_{ \operatorname{Homeo}}$-equivariant.

