 \pi_1 \,{ \operatorname{Homeo}}^+(C_v, C_e)$. Note that $T$ sends the midpoint $b\in B$ (i.e, the image of $h$ in $C_v$) into the midpoint $b'\in B'$ (i.e., the image of $h'$ in $C_e$). Therefore we have a distinguished component $\Pi_0$ of $\Pi$ containing paths $(T_s)$ such that $T_s(B)$ meets $B'$ for all $s$. The transport of $\widetilde T$ along any path from $\Pi_0$ gives the component $\widetilde\Phi$. This concludes the argument.

Assume that $(S,M)$ carries a $\mathbb{G}$-structure. We then have a functor 
\begin{equation} \rho: P(S,M) \longrightarrow {\Delta \mathfrak{G}}-\operatorname{Graph}
\end{equation}
which chooses for a graph $(\Gamma,\gamma)$ in every isotopy class and associates to it the
${\mathcal G}$-structure on $\Gamma$ induced from the embedding $\gamma$ (Proposition \ref
{prop:emb-graph-str}).

\underbar{(a) Oriented case } $G=SO(2)$. In this case, the statement (that the usual moduli space of stable marked {\em Riemann surfaces} has an orbicell decomposition labelled by ribbon graphs) is well known, see, e.g., \cite{arbarello, penner:book} and references therein. We indicate here the main steps which we later analyze to deduce the general case. First, each component of $\mathcal{M}^{SO(2)_{\operatorname{conf}}}$ is a quotient groupoid \[ \mathcal{M}^{SO(2)_{\operatorname{conf}}}(S,M) \,\,=\,\, \operatorname{Mod}^+(S,M) igl ackslash \hskip -.1cm  igl ackslash\operatorname{Teich}^+(S,M). \] Second, $\operatorname{Teich}^+(S,M)$ has a $\operatorname{Mod}^+(S,M)$-invariant regular cell decomposition (triangulation) constructed by Harer \cite{harer} and described in Appendix ef {subsub:harer}. Denote this cell decomposition by ${\mathcal Y}$. The simplices of ${\mathcal Y}$ correspond to tesselations of $(S,M)$ or, dually, to isotopy classes of spanning graphs for $(S,M)$. Inclusions of simplices correspond to coarsenings of tesselations or, dually, to contractions of spanning graphs. Any spanning graph for $(S,M)$ carries an induced ribbon structure. Therefore, orbicells in the quotient orbicell decomposition, which we denote $\mathcal{X}_{S,M}$, form a full subcategory in $\Lambda-\operatorname{Graph}$ with objects those graphs that appear as spanning graphs for $(S,M)$. Finally, each ribbon graph $\Gamma$ gives rise to an oriented marked surface $(S,M)$ obtained by ``thickening" $\Gamma$, so that $\Gamma$ becomes a spanning graph for $(S,M)$, see, e.g., \cite{HSS-triangulated} \S 3.3.4. This shows that for $\mathcal{X}=\coprod_{(S,M)} \mathcal{X}_{S,M}$, we have ${\operatorname{Cell}}(\mathcal{X})\simeq {\Lambda}-\operatorname{Graph}$. Note that Costello \cite{costello-dual} introduced another topological groupoid $\mathcal{N}$ with an orbicell decomposition labelled by ${\Lambda}-\operatorname{Graph}$ which is homotopy equivalent, but not homeomorphic to $\mathcal{M}^{SO(2)_{\operatorname{conf}}}$. \vskip .3cm \underbar{(b) Unoriented case} $G=O(2)$. It is treated similarly to the oriented case, using the identification \[ \mathcal{M}^{O(2)_{\operatorname{conf}}}(S,M) \,\,=\,\,\operatorname{Mod}(S,M)  igl ackslash \hskip -.1cm  igl ackslash \operatorname{Teich}(S,M) \] and the triangulation ${\mathcal Y}$ of $\operatorname{Teich}(S,M)$ described in \S ef{subsub:harer}. As before, the simplices of ${\mathcal Y}$ correspond to triangulations of $(S,M)$ and give M"obius graphs as $\operatorname{Mod}(S,M)$-orbits. Further, any M"obius graph $\Gamma$ can be ``thickened" producing an unoriented surface $(S,M)$ in which it is spanning. So, for the resulting quotient orbicell decomposition $\mathcal{X}$ of $\mathcal{M}^{O(2)_{\operatorname{conf}}}$ we have ${\operatorname{Cell}}(\mathcal{X})\simeq {\Xi}-\operatorname{Graph}$. Note that an analog of Costello's approach \cite{costello-dual} for Klein surfaces was developed by Braun \cite{braun}. \vskip .3cm \underbar{(c) General case:} $G$ an arbitrary planar Lie group. Depending on whether $G$ preserves orientation or not, we consider one of the two projections \[ \pi: \mathcal{M}^{G_{\operatorname{conf}}} \longrightarrow \mathcal{M}^{SO(2)_{\operatorname{conf}}}, \quad \pi: \mathcal{M}^{G_{\operatorname{conf}}} \longrightarrow \mathcal{M}^{O(2)_{\operatorname{conf}}} \] obtained by forgetting the extra structure. In each case, the projection $\pi$ is an unramified covering of orbifolds, so the preimage of any orbicell in the target is a disjoint union of orbicells in the source. We thus obtain an orbicell decomposition of $\mathcal{M}^{G_{\operatorname{conf}}}$ which we now analyze. We sketch the case when $G$ does not preserve the orientation, the other case being similar but easier. Denote by $\mathcal{X}$ the orbicell decomposition of $\mathcal{M}^{O(2)_{\operatorname{conf}}}$ and by $\mathcal{X}^G$ the preimage decomposition of $\mathcal{M}^{G_{\operatorname{conf}}}$. Since any $G_{\operatorname{conf}}$-structured marked surface is $\mathbb{G}$-structured, any tessellation $\mathcal{P}$ of the surface makes the dual graph of $\mathcal{P}$ into a ${\Delta \mathfrak{G}}$-structured graph. We obtain therefore a commutative diagram of categories \[ \xymatrix{ {\operatorname{Cell}}(\mathcal{X}^G) \ar[d]_{q_G} \ar[r]^{\pi} & {\operatorname{Cell}}(\mathcal{X}) \ar[d]^q \ {\Delta \mathfrak{G}}-\operatorname{Graph} \ar[r]_\varpi & {\Xi}-\operatorname{Graph} } \] where $q$ is an equivalence by the case (b) above. We prove that $q_G$ is an equivalence as well. For this, we note that any morphism $\Gamma 	o \Gamma'$ in ${\Delta \mathfrak{G}}-\operatorname{Graph}$ can be expressed uniquely as the composition of a contraction of a set of $I$ of edges of $\Gamma$ (which results in a canonical contraction morphism $\Gamma 	o \Gamma_I$) followed by an isomorphism $\Gamma_I 	o \Gamma'$. This implies that, assuming $\operatorname{Hom}_{{\Delta \mathfrak{G}}-\operatorname{Graph}}(\Gamma,\Gamma') 
e \emptyset$, any fiber of the map \[ \operatorname{Hom}_{{\Delta \mathfrak{G}}-\operatorname{Graph}}(\Gamma,\Gamma') \longrightarrow \operatorname{Hom}_{{\Xi}-\operatorname{Graph}}(\varpi(\Gamma),\varpi(\Gamma')) \] is acted upon simply transitively by the group $\operatorname{Aut}
